WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.780
132 sites lost 24.6% of right-angle crashes and gained 14.9% more rear-end collisions. This

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:13.780
is unintended consequences, episode 165. What follows is the case for targeting deadly

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:18.540
side impacts, the Northern Virginia rollout, the rear-end trade-off that showed up in

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:23.700
the same corridors, how agencies narrowed the tool, and what a city might watch for before

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:29.160
scaling a new enforcement device. In 2005, the Federal Highway Administration examined

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.840
and camera-equipped intersections in seven United States jurisdictions.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:38.580
The same report calculated a modest net economic benefit once crash costs were tallied.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.120
A safety device installed to prevent deadly side-impact crashes produced a measurable increase

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.820
in another crash type at many of the same sites.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.820
By the late 1990s, red light running killed more than 800 people a year in the United States

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:58.780
and injured roughly 200,000 others, according to figures cited in the 2007 Virginia

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:04.280
a Transportation Research Council report. Transportation engineers and public safety officials

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.460
in several states looked at that toll and saw a violation that was easy to name and hard to staff.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.700
Automated cameras looked like a practical way to enforce signals without placing an officer

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:21.060
at every intersection. Jurisdiction such as Alexandria, Arlington, Fairfax County, Falls Church,

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.860
and Vienna in northern Virginia began installing cameras after the state legislature authorized their

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:32.940
use. The technology appeared to offer continuous enforcement at lower staffing costs. It targeted

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:38.060
a specific act, entering an intersection after the light turned red. The violation tied

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:43.060
to the most severe angle collisions. Early studies that did not adjust for regression to the

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:48.160
mean suggested large safety gains, and those results reinforced the decision to expand the

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.340
programs. Officials reason that if a camera could change driver behavior at the exact moment

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.740
the light changed, the most destructive crashes would decline even if total crash volume stayed the

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:04.100
same or rose slightly. That reasoning treated the angle crash as the harm worth buying down,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:10.940
and treated a small rise in other crashes as an acceptable remainder. Between 1998 and 2004,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.840
the six northern Virginia jurisdictions operated red light cameras at selected intersections and

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:22.500
collected seven years of crash data for later analysis. Proponents pointed to immediate drops in

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.540
and red light violations captured on video.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.800
The Federal Highway Administration launched a larger multi-jurisdictional evaluation that

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.520
applied empirical Bay's methods, seeking to isolate the camera's effect from background

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:34.940
trends.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.280
Early press coverage and agency statements emphasized the reduction in angle crashes as

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.580
evidence that the cameras were working as intended.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.460
A smaller number of engineers and statisticians warned that prior evaluations had not controlled

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.700
for regression to the mean.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:58.140
A statistical artifact that can exaggerate apparent safety improvements when sites are chosen because those sites recently experienced high crash counts.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:10.200
The Virginia program therefore became one of the first large-scale tests in which both the intended angle crash reduction and any offsetting rear end increase could be measured against the same baseline data set.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:18.760
Drivers approaching camera equipped intersections break more abruptly to avoid a citation and following vehicles sometimes could not stop in time.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:24.620
The Virginia Transportation Research Council's 2007 analysis of the same northern Virginia

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:30.660
corridors found rear end crashes rose between 27 and 42 percent depending on the statistical

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.980
method with substantial variation across jurisdictions.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.540
In Arlington, every crash category studied increased.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.280
In Vienna, most categories declined.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:46.100
When comprehensive crash costs were calculated, the aggregate result across the six jurisdictions

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:50.660
operations showed a net increase in total crash costs, although injury-only costs fell

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.720
modestly once three fatal angle crashes were set aside.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:59.560
The Campbell Collaborations' 2020 systematic review of 38 studies confirmed an average

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:04.760
19% rise in rear-end collisions once regression to the mean adjustments were applied.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.260
The causal chain ran directly from the enforcement incentive.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.880
The camera punished red light entry and rewarded a sudden stop, shifting crash type rather

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:14.940
than eliminating harm.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:15.040
term.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.460
Second-order effects included higher repair costs for property damage crashes, and occasional

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.640
disputes over camera calibration and yellow interval timing.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:28.680
Rear-end collisions at these sites were more often low-speed events involving two vehicles

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.720
already in the same lane, so average injury severity stayed lower than the angle crashes

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.400
those rear-end collisions replaced.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:41.040
The increase in frequency still raised total repair bills and insurance claims in several jurisdictions.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:48.740
One jurisdiction recorded an annual increase of $140,883 in comprehensive crash costs.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.380
Another recorded an annual reduction of $92,367.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:59.240
The same camera technology produced opposite net outcomes depending on local traffic volume,

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:01.560
signal timing, and approach geometry.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.500
The Virginia Transportation Research Council concluded that cameras should be evaluated

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:13.020
intersection by intersection rather than adopted as a blanket policy. Some jurisdictions reduced or

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:18.840
removed cameras where rear end increases outweighed angle crash reductions. Other jurisdictions retained

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:24.620
cameras at high-risk angle crash locations. Later national guidance incorporated site-specific

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:31.260
crash-type trade-offs into camera program design. The 2020 Campbell review noted that effects continued

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:37.040
to vary with study quality and local conditions, leaving program managers to weigh local geometry,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.000
signal timing, and traffic volume before deciding on new installations.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:46.720
Over time, several agencies began publishing the full before and after crash matrices for

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.800
each candidate site, so that decision makers could see both the angle crash savings and the

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.120
rear end penalty in the same table before voting on installation.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.480
Selecting intersections from recent red light crash counts can pull a program toward places

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.380
is where a statistical rebound is already likely, and a camera then adds a second behavioral

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.020
shift on top of that selection.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.760
Monetizing every crash type, not only the crash the camera was built to stop, is what makes

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:17.840
a modest net benefit visible at all, because the rear end increase has to be subtracted from

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.420
the angle crash savings before anyone knows whether the ledger improved.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:26.560
Requiring a before and after matrix at each site is a way to refuse a location where the

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.480
rear end penalty already exceeds the angle crash gain.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:34.580
pain. Instead of treating one jurisdiction savings as proof for the next corridor, how

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:39.120
might a city today test a new enforcement tool at a handful of intersections before scaling

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.760
that tool to every corridor that shows the original problem? That's the case for today.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.360
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd

